    "final.course_reflection": "This activity made me more interested in the practical side of robotics because I was able to see how mathematical concepts such as velocity, angular velocity, distance, and radius directly affect a robot's movement. Instead of only writing code, I had to think about how the robot would physically move and how small changes in the calculations could affect the final result. The activity also showed me the importance of testing and reviewing code rather than assuming that an implementation is correct. Using AI to help generate ideas was useful, but I still had to understand the code, check the calculations, and write tests to verify that the motion matched the requirements. This made me see AI more as a tool that can assist with engineering work rather than something that replaces the engineer's judgment. What stood out to me most was the connection between software and the physical world. A small programming decision, such as the sign of angular velocity, changes the direction the robot turns. That connection makes robotics interesting to me because programming, mathematics, and engineering all have to work together for the system to behave correctly. Overall, the activity increased my interest in doing more robotics and computing projects where I can write code and then see its effects on a real or simulated system. This lab was less complicated than the last lab, lab 1. An di feel like most of that had to do with me already ebing familair with the file structure. One thing, my laptio is working harder than usual. It is very warm, and the battery is draining fast. I actually have to work plugged in. I can also hear my fan at times. And apparently, this is because of docker.  Reggardless, the lab was fun and seeing the bot move.",
    "final.synthesis": "One example of a program that is technically correct but could still fail in real life could be a trash bot. We can write all the correct code to make our robot pick up all garbage, and to avaid people or obstacles. However, we could still fail becuase it may incorrectly identify an item, such as a laptop, as grabage. And that could happen if we under-define what counts as garbage. The program executed correclty, but becuase of a lack of evidance it made a wrong decision. The pedestrians do not expect their valuable belongings to be thrown out ever. ",
